An email deliverability service cannot repair a message that was routed to the wrong support queue. For a Node.js gaming contact form, choose the service only after the application owns routing, idempotency, suppression-list checks, template testing and preview, plus domain auth state. Then require any candidate to prove those controls under failure.
TL;DR: treat outbound mail as the last stage of a durable notification pipeline, not as a synchronous API call. Persist one intent, render and inspect it, check suppression at send time, and record a stable delivery attempt key. Domain authentication and DKIM rotation belong in the operational acceptance test. A glossy inbox preview is useful, but it does not compensate for duplicate sends or a player-account appeal landing in the billing queue.
What should an email deliverability service prove beyond template testing?
The bounded production failure in this scenario is plain: a player selects "account access," the classifier changes its answer during a retry, and two workers create notifications for different support queues. Both transactional notifications can be perfectly authenticated and delivered. Operations still has an incident because two teams may act on one case, and the player can receive conflicting replies. I have been paged by missed jobs and duplicate deliveries; those pages change the order of evaluation. I start from the page-worthy invariant: one accepted contact submission produces one case identity and one routing decision. Retries may repeat work, but they must not reconsider that decision or create a second notification intent. This is the idempotency reflex that matters more than a feature matrix. The first design I would reject is direct sending in the request handler. It couples page latency to an external delivery outcome and leaves an awkward ambiguity when the process loses its response. A memory-only queue has a similar gap after restart. The safer boundary is a Postgres transaction that stores the contact case, chosen queue, template version, and notification intent together. A worker claims that intent later.
Retries lie.
That advice does not apply to every message. A disposable development preview does not need durable delivery semantics, and a security alert may need a separate, deliberately faster path. Even then, its ownership and duplicate policy should be explicit.
Freeze routing before rendering
For the gaming form, routing inputs might include the selected topic, game identifier, locale, and whether the sender can access the account. Store the normalized inputs and the resulting queue. Do not ask a changing classifier to make the decision again during an email retry.
Postgres can enforce the one-intent boundary with a unique idempotency key. The worker below sketches the preventative path. The transport is an interface on purpose: the reliability contract belongs to the application, while the delivery implementation remains replaceable.
package notification
import (
    "context"
    "errors"
    "time"
)
type Intent struct {
    ID              string
    CaseID          string
    Queue           string
    TemplateVersion string
    Recipient       string
    CreatedAt       time.Time
}
type Store interface {
    Claim(ctx context.Context) (Intent, error)
    IsSuppressed(ctx context.Context, recipient string) (bool, error)
    MarkSuppressed(ctx context.Context, id string) error
    MarkDelivered(ctx context.Context, id, providerRef string) error
    Release(ctx context.Context, id string, retryAt time.Time) error
}
type Transport interface {
    Send(ctx context.Context, idempotencyKey string, message Rendered) (string, error)
}
type Rendered struct {
    To      string
    Subject string
    HTML    []byte
    Text    []byte
}
var ErrTemporary = errors.New("temporary delivery failure")
An idempotency key should identify the notification intent, not an individual worker attempt. Otherwise attempt two looks new to the transport. The database also needs a claim lease or an equivalent recovery rule so a worker crash does not strand the row forever. Keep the retry schedule bounded and observable; an infinite retry loop is just an unbounded queue with a nicer name.
There is a subtle ordering issue. The suppression list must be checked when the worker is about to send, rather than only when the form is submitted. A recipient can become suppressed while an intent waits behind a backlog. Record that terminal decision against the intent so operators can distinguish "not attempted by policy" from "transport failed."
Make templates deployable artifacts
Template testing has three separate jobs: validate the data contract, inspect the rendered MIME alternatives, and review how representative clients display the result. Collapsing them into one screenshot creates false confidence.
Mustache is deliberately logic-less: variables, sections, inverted sections, comments, partials, and related constructs have defined rendering behavior. That makes a small contract test practical. Build fixtures for the shortest and longest plausible player names, a missing optional game title, escaped user text, and every supported locale. Fail the build if a required field renders empty or if unresolved template markers remain. User-controlled contact text must never become trusted template source.
Preview both HTML and plain text from the exact committed template version. Store that version on the intent. If a rollout changes wording while old work is queued, the worker should not silently render yesterday's case with today's template.
The useful preview review is narrow and repeatable: subject, sender identity, reply path, links, accessible reading order, wrapping, and the text alternative. Keep approved fixtures in source control. Screenshots help humans spot layout regressions, but assertions catch missing case IDs every time.
Test authentication as an operational change
Domain authentication is not a one-time checkbox. Evaluate whether a candidate exposes domain auth status clearly enough for a deployment gate and supports a controlled DKIM key rotation in which the old and new records can overlap. The exact DNS procedure depends on the service, so the acceptance test should use the service's documented state rather than guess from a successful API response.
Rotate in a staging sending domain first. Publish the new record, wait for the candidate to report it ready, send controlled messages, then move production traffic through the documented transition. Retire the old record only after the transition condition is satisfied. Keep a rollback point.
No drama. The dangerous version is deleting the old key first and discovering later that resolvers or the delivery configuration have not converged.
Authentication evidence also belongs beside the send record: sending domain, template version, intent ID, and provider reference. Do not log message bodies or raw contact-form secrets merely because debugging is easier. An operator usually needs correlation, state, and timestamps, not a player's private account narrative.
Compare services with failure drills
A neutral selection process is a scored exercise against your own pipeline. Ask every candidate to process the same sanitized fixtures and the same induced failures. Weight delivery reliability most heavily, but define it through recoverable behavior rather than a claimed aggregate rate.
| Drill | Evidence to capture | Reject when | 
|---|---|---|
| Duplicate worker attempt | One intent ID and final outcome | Two user-visible messages are accepted without a deduplication control | 
| Suppression added during backlog | Terminal suppressed state | A queued message bypasses the latest policy decision | 
| Template version changes | Rendered version matches intent | Retry silently switches content | 
| DKIM rotation | Documented readiness and overlap path | Rotation requires an unauthenticated gap | 
| Timeout after submission | Searchable provider reference or safe retry rule | Outcome cannot be reconciled | 
Also inspect exportability, retention controls, webhook authenticity, access boundaries, rate-limit behavior, and support escalation. Cost matters as a capacity input, but it is not the thesis. Model normal volume, a launch-day spike, retries, previews, and log retention; then check that the operating model still makes sense.
Run the drill in deployment, not only during procurement. A template change should execute contract fixtures and previews. A routing change should replay a fixed corpus of sanitized cases and show the queue diff. A domain change should stop if authentication readiness is absent. These are release gates, and each gate needs a named owner.
The worker's control flow should make ambiguous outcomes visible:
func Deliver(ctx context.Context, store Store, tx Transport, render func(Intent) (Rendered, error)) error {
    intent, err := store.Claim(ctx)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    blocked, err := store.IsSuppressed(ctx, intent.Recipient)
    if err != nil {
        return store.Release(ctx, intent.ID, time.Now().Add(time.Minute))
    }
    if blocked {
        return store.MarkSuppressed(ctx, intent.ID)
    }
    message, err := render(intent)
    if err != nil {
        return err // A deterministic render failure needs operator action, not a hot retry loop.
    }
    ref, err := tx.Send(ctx, intent.ID, message)
    if errors.Is(err, ErrTemporary) {
        return store.Release(ctx, intent.ID, time.Now().Add(5*time.Minute))
    }
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    return store.MarkDelivered(ctx, intent.ID, ref)
}
In a real implementation, the timeout case deserves its own state because the provider may have accepted the message even though the worker did not receive the response. Reconcile by the stable intent key or provider reference before retrying. If a candidate cannot support that investigation, the application must choose a conservative policy and document the duplicate-versus-delay trade-off.
Operate the queue, not the dashboard
Alert on user impact and stuck state: oldest ready intent, claim age, retry age, terminal failure count, suppression decisions, and the gap between accepted cases and created intents. Break those signals down by support queue and template version. A global success chart can hide that every account-recovery notification is failing while routine feedback mail continues.
The runbook should answer four questions quickly. Which case and intent are affected? Is the routing decision frozen? Was the recipient suppressed at attempt time? Is the outcome failed, accepted, delivered, or ambiguous? Replaying should reuse the original identity and template version unless an operator explicitly creates a replacement with an audit reason.
This is the selection conclusion: choose the service that survives these drills and gives operators enough evidence to reconcile uncertainty. Keep routing and notification identity in Postgres, keep templates versioned, and keep suppression at the send boundary. Delivery starts before the network call.
